Verify that the settings are applied:

```sql
SHOW VARIABLES LIKE 'performance_schema';
SHOW VARIABLES LIKE 'max_digest_length';
SHOW VARIABLES LIKE 'performance_schema_max_digest_length';
SHOW VARIABLES LIKE 'performance_schema_max_sql_text_length';
```

Expected result: `performance_schema` is `ON` and the three length settings are `4096`.

Create the `db-o11y` monitoring user and grant its base privileges. Replace `YOUR_DB_O11Y_PASSWORD` with a strong password:

```sql
CREATE USER 'db-o11y'@'%' IDENTIFIED BY 'YOUR_DB_O11Y_PASSWORD';
GRANT PROCESS, REPLICATION CLIENT ON *.* TO 'db-o11y'@'%';
GRANT SELECT ON performance_schema.* TO 'db-o11y'@'%';
```

Alloy connects as this user. If you choose a different user name, use it in every later step.

Allow Alloy to manage Performance Schema consumers and actor tracking. Database Observability needs the `events_statements_cpu`, `events_waits_current`, and `events_waits_history` consumers enabled, and it needs the monitoring user's own queries excluded from tracking. Both settings reset when MySQL restarts, so the recommended approach is to let Alloy maintain them:

```sql
GRANT UPDATE ON performance_schema.setup_consumers TO 'db-o11y'@'%';
GRANT INSERT, UPDATE ON performance_schema.setup_actors TO 'db-o11y'@'%';
```

You'll turn on the matching Alloy settings in the next milestone. If you'd rather not let Alloy modify Performance Schema, enable the consumers and disable tracking manually instead and re-apply them after each restart — refer to [Set up Database Observability for MySQL](https://grafana.com/docs/grafana-cloud/monitor-applications/database-observability/set-up/mysql/mysql/).
